This is a full documentation of the APIs available on Edge. <br> 
In order to test the APIs, authentication is required. <br><br>

To generate the secret key needed for the authentication you need to get the hashed password using the Secret Key Generation Steps.

  Secret Key Generation Steps: <br>
- Your static SALT suffix string is “QGFjdGl2ZQ==”. <br>
- Hash (with SHA256) the password concatenated with the salt sha256 ([Password] + SALT).

<br>Please find below a Java code example:
```
 private static String hashMessage(String message) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException {
  MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
  md.reset();
  byte[] buffer = message.getBytes();
  md.update(buffer);
  byte[] digest = md.digest();

  String hexStr = "";
  for (byte aDigest : digest) {
      hexStr += Integer.toString((aDigest & 0xff) + 0x100, 16).substring(1);
  }
  return hexStr;
  }

  private static String makeSecret(String key) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException {
      final String salt = "QGFjdGl2ZQ==";
      return hashMessage(key + salt);
  }

  //This will be the secret key
  final String secretKey = makeSecret("password");
```
<br><b>Basic Authentication</b><br>
This section shows you how to allow REST clients to authenticate themselves using basic authentication (user name and password). This is one of three methods that you can use for authentication against the Edge REST API; the others being basic cookie-based authentication and OAuth2 authentication. In this section, we will show you a simple example of basic authentication.

  <i>SIMPLE EXAMPLE</i><br>
  Most client software provides a simple mechanism for supplying a user name and password and will build the required authentication headers automatically. For example you can specify the -u argument with curl as follows:
  ```
  curl -D- -u admin:5b21665fc7c906c1f8de64dc0534d41a673dbb4b2c76364faf2874d10acba66d -X GET -H “Content-Type: application/json” http://10.0.0.181/ALE/api/logical-device?fields=name
  ```
  <i>SUPPLYING BASIC AUTHENTICATION HEADERS</i><br>
  If you need to you may construct and send basic authentication headers yourself. To do this you need to perform the following steps:

   - Generate a secret key for a specific user following the Secret Key Generation Steps.
   - Build a string of the form username:secret
   - Base64 encode the string
   - Supply an “Authorization” header with content “Basic ” followed by the encoded string. For example, you would make the request as follows: 
  ```
  curl –D- -X GET -H “Authorization: Basic {Base64 Encoded String}” -H “Content-Type: application/json” http://10.0.0.181/ALE/api/logical-device?fields=name
  ```
  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Example: 
  ```
  curl –D- -X GET -H "Authorization: Basic YWRtaW46NWIyMTY2NWZjN2M5MDZjMWY4ZGU2NGRjMDUzNGQ0MWE2NzNkYmI0YjJjNzYzNjRmYWYyODc0ZDEwYWNiYTY2ZA==" -H "Content-Type: application/json" http://10.0.0.181/ALE/api/logical-device?fields=name
  ```  
  <br><b>Basic Cookie-based Authentication</b><br>
  This section shows you how to allow REST clients to authenticate themselves using cookies. This is one of three methods that you can use for authentication against the Edge REST API; the other methods being basic authentication and OAuth2 authentication. This is how basic cookie-based authentication works in Edge at a high level:

  - The client generates a secret key for a specific user following the Secret Key Generation Steps.<div style="color:red">asdasd</div>
  - The client posts to ```http://{{ip}}/ALE/api/auth ``` the username and the secret key generated in step 1 as the password. After that you’ve created a session, it’s just a matter of setting the cookie in all subsequent requests to the server.
  - Store the session object on the client. The way that you do this will depend on how your client is implemented.
   - When you want to make a request, take cookie name and value from the session and use them to set the ‘cookie’ field in the header of your request. This will be used as client session identification.

   <br><b>OAuth2 Authentication</b><br>
   This section shows you how to allow REST clients to authenticate themselves using OAuth2. This is one of three methods that you can use for authentication against the Edge REST API; the other methods being basic authentication and basic cookie-based authentication. This is how OAuth2 authentication works in Edge at a high level:
   - The client generates a secret key for a specific user following the Secret Key Generation Steps.
   - The client posts to ```http://{{ip}}/ALE/api/oauth``` the username and the secret key generated in step 1 as the password. Here is an example of how the request body should look:
  ```
  {
  "username": "admin",
  "password": "5b21665fc7c906c1f8de64dc0534d41a673dbb4b2c76364faf2874d10acba66d"
  }
  ```  
   - The response body will contain both the access token which grants clients access to additional APIs, and the refresh token which allows clients to obtain a new access token when needed. Clients should include the access token in the Authorization header using the Bearer authentication method when making subsequent API requests. Here is an example of how the response body will look:
  ```
  {
  "user":"admin",
  "token_type":"bearer",
  "expires_in":3600000,    
  "access_token":"eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiJhZG1pbiIsImV4cCI6MTcwNzQwNjM0MiwiYXVkIjoiYWNjZXNzIn0.frwZ-U4D0SeucBNZEnpHpXUcyoqq2yLyeFulbNSSjmc",
  "refresh_token":"eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiJhZG1pbiIsImV4cCI6MTcwNzQxMzU0MiwiYXVkIjoicmVmcmVzaCJ9.Wnyc1XDp_KOeaqzcF7Q6JhWbgPLPTOcLOa5-K_cu_b0"
  }
  ```  

   - In the event of an expired access token, clients can obtain a new one by making a request to ```http://{{ip}}/ALE/api/oauth/refresh```. The refresh token should be included both in the request body and the Authorization header using the Bearer authentication method , this ensures the reception of a new access token in the response body. Here is an example of how the request body will look:

   ```
   {
   "refresh_token":"eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiJhZG1pbiIsImV4cCI6MTcwNzQxMzU0MiwiYXVkIjoicmVmcmVzaCJ9.Wnyc1XDp_KOeaqzcF7Q6JhWbgPLPTOcLOa5-K_cu_b0"
   }
  ```

